  "body": "The maintainer asked for this in chat on 2026-11-15, as an enhancement with priority P2.\n\nHis words: \"tools should have its own panels.. not live in \"properties\". If i select a tool in the browser, the settings of that tool should show in properties rather than the tools catalog and \"support features\"\"\n\nWhat happens: when the user selects a tool in the browser (TOOLS or the setup's Tool list), Properties shows the tool catalog and the support features. The settings of the selected tool are not the first thing that shows.\n\nExpected:\n\n1. Select a tool in the browser: Properties shows the settings of that tool only (geometry, holder, cutting data, numbers), as it does for an operation or a setup.\n2. The tool catalog (library browse, import, the support features) moves to a panel of its own. The user opens it from the Tools ribbon button or the browser. It does not occupy Properties.\n3. Each edit of a tool setting goes through the command history (Everything Is a Command), as today.\n\nFor the work:\n\n- List what the current tool page in Properties holds, and decide for each part: tool settings (stays in Properties) or catalog (moves to the new panel). Record the split in `docs/decisions/`.\n- The new panel docks as the other panels do and has a structural test (AccessKit labels and roles), not a pixel snapshot.\n- The PR body has before and after screenshots.\n- If an MCP tool or a script verb opens the catalog page by name, update it.\n\nDone means:\n\n- A click on a tool in the browser shows that tool's settings in Properties.\n- The catalog and the support features are in their own panel, opened from the Tools button.\n- Structural tests pin the two behaviours.\n",
